 sit diagonally at the lower corners. The other colour is scattered as single dots.\n\n**What changes.** The output only adds cells of the dot colour. Every addition is a vertical line that runs down to the bottom edge. Each line passes over any dots in its way.\n\n**Which columns get a line.** I compared the line columns with the dot positions:\n- **Pair 1:** the umbrella spans columns 4–10. Below it there are dots at (10,9) and (12,6). Lines appear in exactly columns 9 and 6.\n- **Pair 2:** the umbrella spans columns 6–12. The only dot below it inside that span is (13,9). There is exactly one line, in column 9. The dots at (10,1), (19,1) and (18,15) are outside the span and cause nothing.\n- **Pair 3:** the umbrella spans columns 9–15. Dots below it inside the span are (9,10), (12,13), (16,12) and (18,13). Lines appear in columns 10, 12 and 13. Columns 12 and 13 each have one line even though column 13 has two dots.\n\nSo a column gets a line if it lies within the umbrella's horizontal span and contains a dot below the umbrella.\n\n**Where a line starts.** It starts on the cell just below the lowest umbrella cell in that column. For example, in pair 3 column 12 the umbrella occupies rows 4–5, so the line starts at row 6 (the notch under the apex). Column 13's lowest umbrella cell is at row 6, so its line starts at row 7. The line then runs to the bottom border.\n\n**Test input.** The umbrella colour is 1 and spans columns 3–9, with its lowest cells at row 6. Dots of colour 6 below it fall in columns 4, 5, 7 and 9:\n- (7,7) gives a line in column 7. Its lowest umbrella cell is at row 5 (the notch), so the line starts at row 6.\n- (8,4) gives a line in column 4, starting at row 6.\n- (10,5) gives a line in column 5, starting at row 6.\n- (16,7) is also in column 7, which already has a line.\n\nColumn 9 needs a check. Its foot is at row 6, so any line there would start at row 7. The only colour-6 cell in that column is at y=0, which is above the umbrella, so column 9 gets no line. The program uses the \"dot below the shape's lowest cell in that column\" test, which handles both cases.\n\n**Identifying the colours in code.** The umbrella colour is the colour of the largest object. The dot colour is the other non-background colour.\n\n**Teaching stages.** The program emits the canvas once after each line is drawn, and then emits the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    shape_col = max(objs, key=lambda o: o[\"size\"])[\"colour\"]\n    cols = [c for c in np.unique(g) if c != bg and c != shape_col]\n    out = g.copy()\n    if not cols:\n        emit(out, \"No dot colour present; nothing to draw.\")\n        return out\n    dot = cols[0]\n    ys, xs = np.where(g == shape_col)\n    H = g.shape[0]\n    for x in range(xs.min(), xs.max() + 1):\n        colys = ys[xs == x]\n        if len(colys) == 0:\n            continue\n        low = colys.max()\n        if not np.any(g[low + 1:, x] == dot):\n            continue\n        out[low + 1:H, x] = dot\n        emit(out, f\"Column {x} lies under the shape and has a {dot} dot below it: \"\n                  f\"drop a {dot} line from row {low+1} to the bottom.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid: every column under the shape with a dot beneath now has a line.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Identifying the colours in code.** The umbrella colour is the colour of the largest object."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Dots of colour 6 below it fall in columns 4, 5, 7 and 9:"}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"e6a431b706e4060a18f4e6187ce9ef75233b8965b8a77cde412b0a60dd366eb2","task_sha256":"b06bdac8e87eeceb9edbf0db1232b0f9b8dc066404a57b91bfdc607c86c70b93","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
